d3d11/dxgi/wined3d: surfaces shared between devices

Office's UI compositor -- it calls itself AirSpace -- renders its text and icons
on one D3D device and composites them on another, through a texture created
with D3D11_RESOURCE_MISC_SHARED_KEYEDMUTEX.  Wine had none of the three pieces
that needs, and Office named the result itself: forty
`.?AVDeviceError@AirSpace@@` C++ exceptions per dialog, exactly matching forty
stubbed GetSharedHandle calls and forty E_NOINTERFACEs for IID_IDXGIKeyedMutex.

  wined3d   a process-global handle registry.  wined3d_resource_get_shared_handle
            creates a kernel handle for a resource and remembers it,
            wined3d_resource_from_shared_handle resolves it back, and
            resource_cleanup drops the entry.  Both dxgi and d3d11 link wined3d,
            so this is the one place both sides reach without new plumbing.

  dxgi      IDXGIResource::GetSharedHandle uses it.  IDXGIKeyedMutex exists at
            last -- Wine declares it in dxgi.idl and implemented nothing.

  d3d11     OpenSharedResource (D3D11 and D3D10) resolves the handle, and
            shared.c carries the surface: each participant gets a texture on its
            own device, they share one keyed-mutex state, and the content moves
            between them at the point the mutex already defines -- whoever
            acquires gets the pixels of whoever released last.

The reason each participant needs its own texture is worth stating, because the
shorter route looks like it works.  wined3d cannot give two devices one piece of
storage: a wined3d texture's GL object belongs to the context that created it,
and handing the same texture to a second device makes **every call succeed while
the pixels quietly do not cross**.  tools/d3dshare in the wine-altars tree is
sixty lines that measure exactly this -- device B clears the surface red, device
A reads it back -- and it took the aliasing shortcut off the table in one run
after it had already cost a day.

So the content is moved out through a staging texture on one device and in
through a staging texture on the other.  That is a round trip through system
memory per handoff.  It is the honest price of not having shared storage, and it
is paid only by surfaces an application actually shares.

Measured, reading pixels off the screen (xwd -root, not the window's own
drawable -- see below):

                                        before      after
    Word start screen, distinct colours      8       ~1300
    SETLANG dialog,    distinct colours    577        1263
    C++ exceptions from Office              43           3
    IDWriteTextLayout::Draw                  0          32
    glyph runs rasterised                    4          78
    D3D11 DrawIndexed                        8         188

Before this, Word showed an empty grey frame with one blank card and no text at
all, and Office's Language Preferences dialog showed panels and borders with
every label, list entry and button caption missing.  Both now render completely.

Two things this does not fix, both verified pre-existing by building without it:
Word's licensing dialog is still a black rectangle, and Word fails to open a
window at all on roughly one start in three.

Note for anyone measuring this: `import -window`/`xwd -id` on the top-level
reads Wine's own window surface, which is GDI content, not what is on the
screen.  The GL child window composited over it is invisible to that read.  Use
`xwd -root` and crop.
